---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CardGrid from '../../../components/CardGrid.astro';
import DocCard from '../../../components/DocCard.astro';
const sidebar = [
  { title: 'Documentation Home', href: '/docs' },
  { title: 'Operations Overview', href: '/docs/operations' },
  { title: 'Two-Host', href: '/docs/operations/two-host' },
  { title: 'Single-Host', href: '/docs/operations/single-host', active: true },
  { title: 'Hardening', href: '/docs/operations/hardening' },
  { title: 'Runbooks', href: '/docs/operations/runbooks' },
];
const toc = [
  { id: 'ids', text: 'Dashboard IDs', level: 2 },
  { id: 'context', text: 'SIEM Single-Host Context', level: 2 },
  { id: 'notes', text: 'Single-Host Notes', level: 2 },
  { id: 'budget', text: 'Resource Budgeting', level: 2 },
];
---
<DocsLayout title="Single-Host Deployment" description="Run the edge and SIEM stacks on one host for evaluation" section="Operations" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <p>
    A single host runs the full lifecycle: proxy, detection, honeypot pools, Suricata, Redis, Elasticsearch
    and Kibana. It is the simplest way to evaluate the system and the right shape for a lab, but the resource
    math changes: every pool and the SIEM compete for the same memory and CPU.
  </p>
  <CardGrid columns={3}>
    <DocCard title="One compose target" icon="box" tag="Simple" description="Start both stacks on one machine and keep all state local. No mTLS channel or remote SSH target is required." meta="Lab friendly" />
    <DocCard title="Shared resources" icon="gauge" tag="Careful" description="Lower POOL_MAX_MEMORY_MB and POOL_MAX_CPUS, and watch the free-memory floor before enabling many pools." meta="Budget first" />
    <DocCard title="Same test caveat" icon="alert" tag="Known limit" description="Loopback traffic can make Suricata attribute alerts to container-internal addresses, so IP reputation may not fire locally." meta="is_private_ip()" />
  </CardGrid>

  <h2 id="ids">Dashboard IDs (All Consolidated)</h2>
  <p>All dashboards are consolidated under a single SIEM instance:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>dashboard-ids-alerts</li>
    <li>dashboard-web-threat-overview</li>
    <li>dashboard-threat-decisions</li>
    <li>dashboard-session-analysis</li>
    <li>dashboard-attack-patterns</li>
    <li>dashboard-threat-intel</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="context">SIEM Single-Host Context</h2>
  <p>All dashboards are consolidated under a single SIEM instance. This configuration handles the full lifecycle of the dashboards, relying on build_dashboards.py for creation and export_dashboards.py for distribution/backup.</p>

  <h2 id="notes">Single-Host Notes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Simplifies deployment by running honeypot, detection, and SIEM components on one host.</li>
    <li>Suitable for evaluation, lab environments, and small-scale deployments.</li>
    <li>May require additional tuning for resource constraints and performance.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>In a single-host deployment, all components (reverse proxy, Nginx Lua pipeline, Suricata, Kibana/Elasticsearch, Redis) run on the same host. The routing pipeline diverts suspicious traffic to local honeypot pools, while security events are logged locally and visualized via Kibana on the same host.</p>

  <h2 id="budget">Resource Budgeting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Set the pool ceiling first.</strong> <code>POOL_MAX</code> and <code>POOL_MAX_MEMORY_MB</code> are the two knobs that keep one busy session from starving Elasticsearch.</li>
    <li><strong>Measure, then lower the reservation.</strong> The pool test measured roughly 430 MB per pool near idle while the default budget charges 1536 MB. Lower <code>POOL_RESERVE_MEM_MB</code> only after confirming a pool behaves on your host.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep the free-memory floor.</strong> <code>POOL_MIN_FREE_MEM_MB</code>, load and disk guards are the last line of defence; do not disable them to squeeze in one more pool.</li>
    <li><strong>Expect slower builds.</strong> With <code>POOL_PARALLEL_BUILDS</code> at 2 and the SIEM sharing the CPU, a fresh pool takes longer to become ready.</li>
    <li><strong>Treat loopback as a test artefact.</strong> Suricata's source address may be container-internal, so validate the network path on a real external request before tuning reputation thresholds.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
